WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.290
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.695 --> 00:00:08.070
ARUN SUBRAMANIYAN:
So today we're

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.170
going to be talking
about democratizing

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.960
AI for industrial applications
and also democratizing

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.070
AI in general.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.040
So I'm Arun Subramaniyan.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.630
I lead data science
and analytics

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.470
for Baker Hughes, a GE company.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.320
I just want to start
off with some challenges

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.320
and potential opportunities
that the oil and gas

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.400
industry presents.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.180
And although the slightest
title has an oil and gas

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.490
industry-specific
slide, this is pretty

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.130
common for most of the
traditional industries that

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.650
are out there today.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.970
Think about the left
hand side of the equation

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.490
where you have assets
systems, very large

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.700
scale critical infrastructure
that has been commissioned

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.730
over the last 30 to 50 years.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:50.580
They are aging.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.500
They're in different
stages of aging,

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.750
different stages of
performance, different stages

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.160
of reliability.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.910
They all need to be somehow
upgraded and kept alive

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:04.140
at 90% or about reliability
and availability.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.440
To make things more complex
or rather interesting,

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.410
a lot of the workforce that
has kept these systems up

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:13.560
and running live and
understand these systems well,

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.020
are slated to retire in
the next 5 to 10 years.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.980
So even if there are
enough engineers who

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:22.363
will get trained and onboarded
into these industries,

00:01:22.363 --> 00:01:24.030
it's very, very hard
for them to come up

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:28.500
to speed and fill the gap that
these experienced engineers

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.652
and scientists and developers
would leave behind.

00:01:31.652 --> 00:01:33.360
So there has to be
something that bridges

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.740
the gap between the two.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:39.180
And the cost of any of these
systems going down, actually

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.590
is astronomical even today.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:43.390
It's scary to think about what
would be the cost 10 or 12,

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:45.060
15 years down the line.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.280
The number down there,
that is $6.6 billion

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.090
in unplanned downtime cost.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:56.350
That is just for downstream
in North America.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:58.110
So if you'd look at
the unplanned downtime

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.600
costs across the
world from upstream,

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.443
that is where you extract oil,
midstream where you transport

00:02:03.443 --> 00:02:05.110
oil, and downstream
where you process it

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:08.550
and then send it off to gasoline
or any of the other petroleum

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:12.040
products, that numbers is
in hundreds of billions,

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.800
if not trillions.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.710
Now that's the challenge
part of the story.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.000
The opportunity side
is on the right side.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.980
Now oil and gas industry has
enjoyed very high oil prices

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:23.900
for a while.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.870
And they also have enjoyed
need or growth of need

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:31.650
across the board,
until about 2012, 2013,

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:37.560
where the oil price went from
$130 a barrel to less than $40

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.420
a barrel overnight.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.750
And what that cost was
a sudden realization

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.420
that the industry was
slightly lagging behind

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.670
compared to the aviation
industry or the power

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.040
industry or any other
traditional industries.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.955
And the need for
sudden digitalization,

00:02:52.955 --> 00:02:55.080
the need for going after
something that they've not

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.682
done, came about very, very
suddenly in the industry.

00:02:58.682 --> 00:03:00.140
And the oil prices
have stabilized,

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:02.010
have gone back
down and up again.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.670
But what it has resulted
in is the top right figure

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:09.750
that you see there, where most
of the industrial companies

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.600
that want to invest
in AI, in any kinds

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.340
of modern technologies, 75%
of the fraction of money

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:18.990
that's going to
be invested in AI

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.390
are invested in
something analytics,

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.360
is going to come from
the oil and gas market.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.270
That's partly driven by the need
and partly driven by the fact

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.250
that it was lagging
behind for some time.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.230
Now they're catching
up quite a bit.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.060
Now flip the equation
a little bit.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.160
And I didn't grow up in
the oil and gas industry.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.410
I actually grew up in the
aviation and power industry,

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:41.340
before coming on to looking at
problems that require analytics

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:42.400
in oil and gas.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.220
And the first thing
that surprised me

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.640
was the scale and quantity
of data in oil and gas.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.520
So compare the volumes
of data here on the slide

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.680
here to what you would
see in aviation or power.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.980
So if you take off from
New York, land in London,

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.260
typically, the aircraft
with active engines

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.750
generates about
terabytes of data.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:04.308
Out of that terabyte of data,
you store maybe 100 megabytes.

00:04:04.308 --> 00:04:05.850
Because it's very
expensive to store,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.980
very expensive to transmit,
and traditionally, it's

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:11.010
not something that the
airline industry has stored.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:15.150
Compare that to the oil and gas
industry, where an offshore oil

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:17.730
rig, an offshore oil
platform generates anywhere

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:22.260
from 20,000 to 30,000 sensors
generating data every two

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.730
to three seconds, stored
in perpetuity since the day

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:30.010
that platform was
commissioned, so 20, 30 years.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:33.090
That's a data scientist bonanza.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:36.090
And if you can actually
get to that data

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:39.360
correctly and do something
about it, that's fantastic.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.400
And that's really where
the opportunity lies.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:46.682
Now I also want to pinpoint
one thing where analytics

00:04:46.682 --> 00:04:49.140
means a lot of different things
to lot of different people.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.980
What it means to us from Baker
Hughes GE and GE in [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:56.340
is the combination of three
very important things.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.733
Analytics is not
necessarily anything new.

00:04:58.733 --> 00:05:00.150
If you look at
engineering, if you

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:04.740
look at any kinds of systems
where a large scale domain

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.500
knowledge that is acquired
a combination of domain

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.518
and traditional software
has been around for decades.

00:05:10.518 --> 00:05:12.310
That's what produces
traditional solutions.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:14.220
That's what runs
the world today.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.310
The new kid on the
block is what today

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:20.460
is known as the cool
parts of data science.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:25.200
Think of your most favorite
algorithm, most common buzzword

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:25.840
out there.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:26.590
Throw it in there.

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.370
That's data science-- combined
with a slightly different way

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.980
of writing software, modern
software on the cloud,

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.080
automatically
scalable, horizontally

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.860
and vertically scalable.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.430
Now that has also
been proven to do

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.170
some things that
are not possible

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:42.000
from the traditional solutions.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.810
Now if any of us were told 10
years ago that a system would

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:49.020
automatically tell you when
to leave the stage today, go

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.810
home, start at 4:30 so that
you can actually get home

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:54.510
on time navigating
all the traffic,

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:57.180
making sure that you're
actually on time to take

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:58.860
your daughter to
her dance class,

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.160
because your calendar knew
that your daughter has a dance

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.640
class, your phone
knows that you're here,

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:08.260
and it knows to navigate
between those two locations,

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:09.870
we all will probably laughed.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:12.240
But that's solved today.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:18.030
Or think of a case where a
shop would send you something

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:21.090
before you even expressed
the intent that you

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:23.930
liked that thing.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:25.170
That's also solved.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:26.750
Like, Amazon's sends
you stuff today

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:29.390
that you never once
liked anywhere.

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:32.240
A little creepy, but it's done.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.770
And that's the needle in a
haystack kind of problem, very,

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:36.170
very hard problems to solve.

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:38.420
But thanks to the Googles
and Amazons and the Facebook

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:40.340
and Apples of the
world, they have shown

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.230
us a way to solve those things.

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:44.810
Now translate that to
the industrial domain.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.330
Most of the time
that 20,000 sensors

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:50.450
transmitting to the three
second date every day

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:53.090
for the last 30 years,
they just tell us

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:55.490
that the systems are fine,
because the systems are

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:59.040
designed to be fine for a
very long period of time.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:01.220
So if you go looking
for something

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:05.270
meaningful in that data, you
most of the time find nothing.

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:08.472
All that data means nothing
to most of the time.

00:07:08.472 --> 00:07:09.930
The one time when
the system fails,

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:11.940
the one time that something
actually goes wrong,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:15.260
you won't have any
data about that thing.

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:16.850
And that's really
what is the problem

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:18.225
that you're trying
to find, where

00:07:18.225 --> 00:07:20.720
we're trying to find a needle
in an ocean of data, not even

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.012
a needle in a haystack problem.

00:07:22.012 --> 00:07:23.510
And most of the
time, we don't even

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:25.235
know what the needle looks like.

00:07:25.235 --> 00:07:27.110
And that's the problem
we're trying to solve.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:29.070
And I'll give you
some examples of that.

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:32.240
But before I go into that one,
I know I introduced myself

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.760
as a head of data science,
but this is a tenant

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.900
that we believe very
deeply us in our teams.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.920
And this is something
attributed to George Box.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:41.510
He said it a long time ago.

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:44.990
"All models are wrong,
but some are useful."

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:46.290
Box stopped there.

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:48.040
Actually we go a little
further than that.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:54.060
"All models are wrong, some
are useful for a short period

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:55.530
of time."

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:57.030
And if you believe
that very deeply,

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:59.373
then you design your systems
very, very differently.

00:07:59.373 --> 00:08:00.790
You design your
teams differently.

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:03.630
You think about problems
and solutions differently.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:06.730
So we don't necessarily
go into a problem saying,

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:09.090
hey, we have this cool
data science team,

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.220
your domain experts or
subject matter experts

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:15.210
will be designing the system and
running it reliably for last 30

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.310
years don't know what we know.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.590
So we'll come solve
the problem for you.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:21.400
We actually rarely do that.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:23.380
Sometimes we do that
and get paid for it.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.950
But most of the time, it's
the standard that drives us.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:27.560
And I'll give you
the reasons why.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.720
And if you think about this
carefully, any model that we

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:33.030
build, even the most
sophisticated deep learning

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:34.980
model that you can
build on thousands

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:39.070
of hours of GPU or TPU compute,
this standard still holds true.

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:41.760
So you have to have systems that
constantly update themselves,

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:43.909
constantly go in and
make sure that they

00:08:43.909 --> 00:08:45.747
are monitoring your models.

00:08:45.747 --> 00:08:47.580
So you need models that
monitor your models.

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:48.997
And then you need
more models that

00:08:48.997 --> 00:08:52.500
monitor your people
who build the models.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.760
So that is where our concept
of digital twin was born.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.440
And most of the
concept on this slide

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:00.393
was born out of pure necessity.

00:09:00.393 --> 00:09:02.310
And so we didn't really
sit in an ivory tower,

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:04.620
think about this, and say
this is a fantastic way

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:05.920
to solve problems.

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:08.040
We were actually
posed with problems

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.230
where things like, OK, there
is one failure in the field.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:13.650
There's one jet
engine that failed.

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:16.890
Can you actually tell
from that one failure

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:19.740
do you have to take down an
entire fleet of engines down,

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:23.520
which causes a massive outrage
in the entire transportation

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:25.350
network in the world or not?

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:27.840
Safety issues where an
offshore oil platform just

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:28.710
sent off an alert.

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.630
Do you shut down
the platform which

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:34.768
cost you about $50 million
a day if you shut it down?

00:09:34.768 --> 00:09:36.310
If you don't shut
it down, it's going

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:38.430
to cause an environmental
disaster that's

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:42.140
going to go costing about
a trillion dollars over 3

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:43.740
to 5 or 10 years.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:46.210
Those are the kinds of answers
you're trying to answer.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:47.627
And most of the
time we don't have

00:09:47.627 --> 00:09:49.390
data to go about
solving the problem.

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:52.080
So the concept of digital
twin where the model

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.350
is a combination of
domain, we most often

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:57.330
just start with
domain, build around

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:58.980
traditional machine
learning problems

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:02.810
that gives us the ability to
solve known problems very well.

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:04.350
So if I tell you
there's a crack,

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:07.590
the crack is propagating
because of a thermal load.

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:09.507
I can give you very,
very, very precise models

00:10:09.507 --> 00:10:11.007
that tell you how
the crack is going

00:10:11.007 --> 00:10:12.510
to propagate under that load.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:14.430
That's the known knowns.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:16.650
Now if I go one
level above and say

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:18.840
I don't necessarily
know the temperatures

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:20.610
that are affecting
this particular part.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.800
I can measure it to within
plus or minus 10 degrees

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.000
or 20 degrees.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:25.560
That's a known unknown.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:28.305
So the known unknown
of temperature,

00:10:28.305 --> 00:10:30.900
I can propagate through and
tell you, well, this part

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:35.280
is going to fail within the next
10 days with a 90% probability.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:38.710
That's where probabilistic
inference comes in.

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:42.080
The real advantage of
using deep learning for us

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:45.020
is where I can't
necessarily pose the problem

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:47.780
in known knowns
or known unknowns,

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:50.430
cases where I know that
something is going to go wrong,

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:52.700
but I don't necessarily
know what to go catch.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:56.360
So I'll go back to the
20,000 sensor example.

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:00.070
We have 20,000 sensors emitting
data every two seconds.

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:03.020
I've written rules,
heuristics, watched all kinds

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:05.000
of critical things already.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:07.580
I can catch maybe 90%
of the things that

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:08.730
are going to go wrong.

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:10.440
What about the last 10%?

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:11.960
I don't know what
rules to write.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:14.180
I don't even know what
to watch, and how often

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:15.442
do I need to watch it for.

00:11:15.442 --> 00:11:17.150
That's really where
deep learning systems

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:17.843
come into play.

00:11:17.843 --> 00:11:19.760
That's how we actually
got into deep learning.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:21.680
And then as we learned
more and started

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:23.702
deploying these systems
more, we realized

00:11:23.702 --> 00:11:25.910
that it can also tell us
when to go build our models.

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:29.210
It can also tell us when
to go watch what models.

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:30.840
So I'll give you some
examples of that.

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:33.020
But before I go there,
I wanted to give you

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:36.500
an example of what we
think about as scale

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:38.800
or systems of systems.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:40.430
So we most of the
time look at systems

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:41.940
that are very, very large.

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:44.270
So that offshore
oil platform has

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:47.670
roughly about 30 different
large scale systems in that.

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:52.670
Each system has anywhere from
10 to 15 large subsystems.

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:57.200
Each subsystem has
50 to 100 assets.

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:01.600
An asset would be something
like a gas turbine.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:03.880
For those of you who have
not seen a gas turbine,

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:08.140
some of the gas turbines fill
rooms that are twice the size.

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:10.600
So very, very large
scale things that are

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:11.860
considered a small asset.

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.193
And they won't even be described
in this particular plot

00:12:14.193 --> 00:12:15.190
on the right side.

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:17.890
And most of the time, the
problem we are trying to solve

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:20.740
is OK, I have data from all
of these different assets, all

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:25.060
of these different sensors
coming in, what is going wrong?

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:27.160
I already know to catch
90% of the things that

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:27.990
are going wrong.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:30.610
Catch that 10% of the
things that I cannot catch.

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:34.340
I, meaning the operator
of the actual platform.

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:36.820
So what we do is we actually
convert the physical system

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:39.110
into an information network.

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:40.583
So we know already
how assets are

00:12:40.583 --> 00:12:42.250
connected, how
subsystems are connected,

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:43.780
how systems are connected.

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:46.030
The connectivity is what is
shown on the right inside.

00:12:46.030 --> 00:12:48.730
So each node there
is actually an asset

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:50.290
that is connecting
to each other,

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:53.290
maybe a pump is connected
to a fuel system,

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:55.218
a fuel system is connected
to a gas turbine,

00:12:55.218 --> 00:12:57.760
a gas turbine would be connected
to a compressor, a whole lot

00:12:57.760 --> 00:12:59.080
of different things.

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:01.670
That physical connectivity
is shown on the right side.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:05.340
But the colors actually
come from the data.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:07.760
So what we do is we
take operational data

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:10.820
and try to understand purely
from operational data,

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:13.040
is there anything
connected with each other

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:14.660
without knowing how
they're connected

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:15.890
in the physical space?

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:17.270
That's the one on the left side.

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:19.470
Now you superimpose that
onto the right hand side,

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:20.803
you see some interesting things.

00:13:20.803 --> 00:13:23.420
You start seeing things
where most of the systems

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:26.857
seem to be connected only with
the assets inside that system.

00:13:26.857 --> 00:13:29.440
So if you look at the blue dots,
they are only in the top left

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:31.730
and maybe things that
are physically connected.

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:34.280
But there are some green
dots on the top right

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:35.930
and on the bottom left.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:37.497
There is no physical connection.

00:13:37.497 --> 00:13:39.080
There's no direct
physical connection.

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:42.813
There's not even a second or
third order direct connection.

00:13:42.813 --> 00:13:44.480
But your operational
data is telling you

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:45.990
that there's
something correlated.

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:48.660
Now this could be
one of two things.

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:50.840
It's the traditional or
the classical problem

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:53.380
of correlation is not causation.

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:55.340
Where this could
very well be hey,

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:58.610
are you trying to tell me that
the S&amp;P 500 index is correlated

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:01.430
to milk prices in Bangladesh?

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:05.000
You can definitely come up with
a data set that tells you that.

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:10.100
Now the proof of this exercise
is can you predict failures

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:13.520
that you cannot predict
before and reliably?

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:14.700
And that is here.

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:16.760
So we've taken
this approach where

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:19.980
we combine the physical network
with the operational history,

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:22.190
connected it together, build
models, very large scale

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:26.840
models, and tell you what these
models are, and predicted what

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:28.680
was not known to us before.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:31.190
So this is a real example
where a customer gave us

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:34.250
operational data for two
years worth of operations

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:35.420
of one subsystem.

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:36.600
Sorry, one system.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:39.140
And that one system had
about 13 subsystems,

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:40.400
roughly 4,000 sensors.

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:42.290
That's what they call
a proof of concept,

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:44.990
about five terabytes of data.

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:46.350
We build our models.

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:49.580
All of these models were a
combination of auto encoders,

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:52.970
probabilistically enabled, and a
system of system network model.

00:14:52.970 --> 00:14:55.540
So all of them combined
together to predict

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:57.830
a 30 years worth of data,
trying to figure out

00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:01.950
if the model can predict things
that it hasn't seen before.

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:04.310
And the proof is we
could successfully detect

00:15:04.310 --> 00:15:06.035
all unplanned downtime events.

00:15:06.035 --> 00:15:08.660
The events that we hadn't seen,
we didn't know how many events.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:11.240
We just predicted
16 out of 16 events.

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:14.010
Now I could have done that
very easily by just saying,

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:16.390
let me call everything
as an event.

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:18.630
I could have caused
1600 failures,

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:21.560
and that would have included the
16 failures that was actually

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:22.950
there.

00:15:22.950 --> 00:15:25.550
So to make sure that a model
was not over tuned or just junk

00:15:25.550 --> 00:15:28.640
or just flipping a
coin, we also made sure

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:31.940
that we can compare it with
the current scenario of how

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:33.260
do you catch faults.

00:15:33.260 --> 00:15:35.660
So we were able to get
all unplanned downtime

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:39.440
events by reducing false
positives and false negatives

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:41.940
simultaneously.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:44.360
And this one we
can do repeatably.

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:46.220
And to do this
repeatably, we actually

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:48.980
have to go through many
different pipelines.

00:15:48.980 --> 00:15:51.020
So if you think about
this, you've got data.

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:52.070
You validate the data.

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:55.200
You walk through say,
an architecture space.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:56.568
You give it to a data scientist.

00:15:56.568 --> 00:15:57.110
They say, OK.

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:58.910
I know for this
particular problem

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:01.190
I can get to a model
that looks good.

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:03.560
They will use a particular
type of autoencoder.

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:05.420
Maybe I use a
particular type of LSTM.

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:07.310
I'll get a model.

00:16:07.310 --> 00:16:09.980
And a data scientist
time is very valuable.

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:12.350
Usually you build maybe one
or two, maybe three or four

00:16:12.350 --> 00:16:14.090
iterations and you stop.

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:17.570
That's a typical workflow
from the left or right.

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:19.950
In our case, because we
didn't have the luxury to say,

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.680
OK, I have a prediction.

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:25.160
You will give me the results
of a blind validation test,

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:27.440
and I can go back
and update my models.

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:28.910
I still have to go
and build models

00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:31.260
without knowing what my
validation data set is,

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:33.050
what the true test data set is.

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:34.910
So we actually had to
go build and explore

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:37.730
the entire architecture space
and come up with models that we

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:42.050
know are not just a model,
but a very, very good model

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:43.322
to begin with.

00:16:43.322 --> 00:16:45.530
So the way we do it is we
actually start with initial

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:48.290
samples that you see here--
those are the red crosses--

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:52.490
where we say we let me go
explore the design space,

00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:55.160
and use that to build
a Bayesian meta model.

00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:57.333
So this is a meta model
that tells you, OK,

00:16:57.333 --> 00:16:59.750
if I change a number of layers,
if I change the activation

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:03.162
functions, do I use
regularization or not.

00:17:03.162 --> 00:17:04.579
That is, if you
decide that you're

00:17:04.579 --> 00:17:07.483
going to use some kind
of a deep neural network.

00:17:07.483 --> 00:17:08.900
Or you can also
go in and say, let

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:10.483
me go will the
Gaussian process model.

00:17:10.483 --> 00:17:14.250
Or I'll just go build a simple
linear regression model.

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:16.880
All of those are
different search spaces

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:18.500
that we're searching across.

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:19.819
Use it to build a better model.

00:17:19.819 --> 00:17:22.130
And then use a genetic
algorithm to go

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:25.859
optimize all of those different
variables simultaneously.

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:28.670
So it's a massive optimization
exercise that goes from the red

00:17:28.670 --> 00:17:32.360
dot through all the grey dots
to get you to those green

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:33.860
[INAUDIBLE] optimal points.

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:36.950
Now typically you may be used
to seeing optimization problems

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:39.480
as going up and
north, as to be good.

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:41.840
Here, the two objectives
are loss functions.

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:43.630
So you want it to be
as small as possible,

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:45.470
reducing loss in
different dimensions

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:47.690
to get it to very,
very small values.

00:17:47.690 --> 00:17:50.570
You get to an objective--
to get here we had

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:52.880
to use TensorFlow at its edge.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:55.670
We had to go back and
use Kubeflow at its edge.

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:57.220
And I'll tell you
how we did that.

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:59.720
And combine all of this with
TensorFlow probability as well.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:02.540
Of course, all of
these running on Nvidia

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:05.970
NGC containers as well.

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:08.830
The nice thing is,
to get to a model,

00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:10.990
we could go explore
more than a quarter

00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:14.580
million architectures very
rapidly for one model.

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:16.910
So we're just going and
building one model here.

00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:20.050
And the improvement in training
from just a random search

00:18:20.050 --> 00:18:21.660
is at least 10 times.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:24.490
And 10 times is what we
can achieve consistently.

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:28.000
So you give it to the best
data scientist we have on team.

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:30.482
They go and find out saying
OK, this model will work

00:18:30.482 --> 00:18:31.690
for this particular use case.

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:33.310
I know I build it on the times.

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:34.630
Let me go build it.

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:35.560
Come back to that.

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:37.060
You still come up
with architectures

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:38.703
that are significantly better.

00:18:38.703 --> 00:18:40.370
Now if you flip the
question and ask us,

00:18:40.370 --> 00:18:41.870
do you understand
the architectures?

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:43.850
Do you understand why
something is working?

00:18:43.850 --> 00:18:45.650
The answer is no.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:47.313
Many times we
actually look at that

00:18:47.313 --> 00:18:48.730
and say why exactly
is it working?

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:49.670
I don't know.

00:18:49.670 --> 00:18:51.410
And I don't really
know if I ever

00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:53.200
picked that particular
architecture,

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:55.500
but it works in this
particular use case.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:58.837
And this is for one model.

00:18:58.837 --> 00:19:01.420
So for the offshore oil platform
that I've been talking about,

00:19:01.420 --> 00:19:04.420
that platform has
about 40 systems.

00:19:04.420 --> 00:19:08.920
Each of the systems would have
about eight of these models.

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:11.830
And so the scale you're talking
about is significantly high.

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:14.410
And to get this to run
properly, of course,

00:19:14.410 --> 00:19:17.140
cost a lot of GPU compute.

00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:19.750
And as most of you
probably know in the Zoom,

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:22.210
GPUs are reasonably expensive.

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:25.550
And running them at this
scale can break the bank.

00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:30.005
Now the general question we
face is are you guys crazy?

00:19:30.005 --> 00:19:31.630
Do you really, really
want to go around

00:19:31.630 --> 00:19:33.580
this many models at scale?

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:36.718
This is a plot we actually
use with our CFOs as well.

00:19:36.718 --> 00:19:38.260
So that's the anomaly
detection model

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:40.600
on the left side that's
actually at scale

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:41.890
from the previous slide.

00:19:41.890 --> 00:19:45.340
The model that Facebook open
sourced just last month,

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:50.260
on OpenGo, the the ELF model,
that model ran on 2,000 GPUs

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:52.480
for two weeks.

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:54.410
So compared to the
scale of operations

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:56.540
that we're talking about
where the model saying

00:19:56.540 --> 00:19:59.330
something wrong can
give you an outcome that

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:02.310
will get the business to
lose tens of millions,

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:06.260
if not hundreds of millions,
actually is very, very limited.

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:09.670
The reason why there's a sticker
shock in these kinds of cases

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:11.930
is we are not used to
building these kinds of models

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:13.072
up to this point in time.

00:20:13.072 --> 00:20:14.780
For these kinds of
operational histories,

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:16.655
we don't really build
these models like that.

00:20:16.655 --> 00:20:18.590
Today, if you ask us
how an offshore oil

00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:20.240
platform is managed,
it's probably

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:22.440
managed with a spreadsheet.

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:25.400
But what is lost in that
spreadsheet calculation is

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:28.490
all of the effort that
goes in, in getting

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:31.065
a simple answer to
the operator, imagine

00:20:31.065 --> 00:20:33.440
what the operator can do with
a model which will tell you

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:36.470
50 days or 100 days before that
something is going to happen,

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:38.270
and you can change
your operations.

00:20:38.270 --> 00:20:40.760
The financial impact
of that is significant.

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:43.280
How to do all of
these things at scale,

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:46.110
we do need to orchestrate
things very, very differently.

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:48.170
So for us, everything
runs inside containers.

00:20:48.170 --> 00:20:50.120
Containers is a
first class citizen.

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:54.540
And it's not just that we get
to build these cool new models.

00:20:54.540 --> 00:20:56.843
We also have to live
in the world of models

00:20:56.843 --> 00:20:58.760
that have existed for
the last 30 years, which

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:00.357
are running reliably.

00:21:00.357 --> 00:21:01.940
Most of the times
we can't necessarily

00:21:01.940 --> 00:21:03.700
go record them or change them.

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:06.370
And most of the time we
don't need to change them.

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:08.370
So the workflow
that's shown here

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:10.250
where you start
with a Python model,

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:13.220
it goes into some proprietary
model say, for example,

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:17.180
[INAUDIBLE],, there's a Fortran
model that feeds something

00:21:17.180 --> 00:21:20.030
into that pipeline that goes
into something else that's

00:21:20.030 --> 00:21:22.850
Python, that might actually
run a robust optimizer in Go,

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:25.410
is actually fairly
common for us.

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:27.320
We don't necessarily
use Fortran 4 that much,

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:31.310
but Fortran 77 is something
we have quite a lot of.

00:21:31.310 --> 00:21:33.768
And that's not just for oil
and gas, any industry as well.

00:21:33.768 --> 00:21:35.810
So you need to be able to
run all of these things

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:38.240
simultaneously at
scale, orchestrated

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:41.370
and get an answer
back to the user.

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:44.390
Now the example I showed
before, where you actually

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:48.610
start with some few
red crosses, that's

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:50.270
the initial points
on the top, there's

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:51.980
an actual Kubeflow
pipeline that's

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:55.220
implemented to run both on
prem as well as on a cloud.

00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:58.820
We'll show you running
this on GCP today.

00:21:58.820 --> 00:22:00.560
That's the initial
model running.

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:02.190
After all of those
models are run,

00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:05.240
Kubeflow decides that OK, let
me go build this Bayesian model,

00:22:05.240 --> 00:22:08.063
and then spun off yet another
set of models after I optimize.

00:22:08.063 --> 00:22:09.230
That's the second iteration.

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:11.480
You keep going to
multiple iterations.

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:14.630
Finally, you get to an
optimal architecture.

00:22:14.630 --> 00:22:17.360
Now the data scientist who
is building these models

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:20.030
does not know much
about Bayesian models

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:21.830
or even optimization.

00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:25.130
The optimization expert
who knows and understands

00:22:25.130 --> 00:22:28.580
stochastic optimization does not
know what models are actually

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:30.520
being optimized.

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:33.580
The software architect who makes
sure that all of these things

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:34.990
are done at scale, GPUs--

00:22:34.990 --> 00:22:37.510
when you require GPUs,
actually are available,

00:22:37.510 --> 00:22:39.460
and all of these
things actually are

00:22:39.460 --> 00:22:42.310
available when a data
scientist kicks off run,

00:22:42.310 --> 00:22:44.740
does not necessarily
understand any of this.

00:22:44.740 --> 00:22:46.990
You need to be able to
orchestrate all of these things

00:22:46.990 --> 00:22:49.180
together to run at scale.

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:51.760
Now to even get there,
we had to make sure

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:55.030
that OK, you can go ask
for 100 GPUs or 1,000 GPUs

00:22:55.030 --> 00:22:57.300
or 100,000 GPUs from GCP.

00:22:57.300 --> 00:22:58.300
They may give it to you.

00:22:58.300 --> 00:23:00.960
And it's a lot of
money on your side

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:04.160
to go get those hardware
actually available to you.

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:06.100
But do you actually
have the software

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:08.292
to go load them up at scale?

00:23:08.292 --> 00:23:10.000
So this is a load
balancer that we wrote.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:12.490
And this is a
result of a test run

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:15.790
where we have two systems
competing with each other.

00:23:15.790 --> 00:23:18.430
Every time the
load balancer sees

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:21.930
that all of these GPUs or all
the resources are consumed,

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:23.700
it will add a node to the GPU.

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:26.060
So that's what's
happening on the top left.

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:29.740
So each time it hits
something, you add a node.

00:23:29.740 --> 00:23:34.300
The actual load or utilization
per GPU is on the bottom left.

00:23:34.300 --> 00:23:37.810
Immediately after a
GPU node is added,

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:40.110
the total utilization goes down.

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:42.440
So there's something else
that's watching that says,

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:44.300
OK, the total utilization
has gone down.

00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:45.640
I actually have more resources.

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:47.220
I can reload them back up.

00:23:47.220 --> 00:23:48.970
So there is a constant
tug of war going on

00:23:48.970 --> 00:23:51.580
between these two systems
where the load drops and it

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:52.750
goes back up.

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:54.960
As soon as it goes back
up, another GPU node

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.340
is added, until of
course you hit a point

00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:59.140
where you can't spend anymore.

00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:01.360
So that's what happens
when you hit two 10 nodes

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:03.070
with two GPUs each.

00:24:03.070 --> 00:24:05.110
So getting up to
this level of scaling

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:07.995
where you had a GPU node,
you get it instantly,

00:24:07.995 --> 00:24:10.120
your billing starts instantly,
but most of the time

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:11.530
you're going to
use it instantly.

00:24:11.530 --> 00:24:13.170
So you have to be
careful about that.

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:15.550
And after you're
done, you need to be

00:24:15.550 --> 00:24:17.980
able to shut it down instantly,
because billing continues

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:19.420
to go.

00:24:19.420 --> 00:24:22.360
So this one, running the
same Kubeflow experiment

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:24.572
that we showed before
is what is shown here.

00:24:24.572 --> 00:24:26.530
So we ran the first
experiment, the load spikes

00:24:26.530 --> 00:24:27.940
on the top left.

00:24:27.940 --> 00:24:31.000
Then the Bayesian optimization
runs, so the load drops.

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:32.660
Then the second
load goes back up.

00:24:32.660 --> 00:24:35.077
An interesting thing that's
happening on the bottom right,

00:24:35.077 --> 00:24:38.050
you can see, is each of
the times it hits max load,

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:39.970
it actually adds more nodes.

00:24:39.970 --> 00:24:42.340
And once all of the
initial runs are done,

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:45.472
it spins it back down way
below where it started.

00:24:45.472 --> 00:24:47.680
And this has to happen
consistently across the board,

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:51.130
because many times we have 10,
20, 30 data scientists running

00:24:51.130 --> 00:24:52.550
these pipelines.

00:24:52.550 --> 00:24:54.430
And we can't
necessarily have SREs

00:24:54.430 --> 00:24:57.970
on standby to keep increasing
GPUs or even watching.

00:24:57.970 --> 00:25:00.850
Do you have the ability
to run GPUs or not?

00:25:00.850 --> 00:25:03.655
I'm picking on GPUs a
bit here, because that's

00:25:03.655 --> 00:25:04.530
reasonably expensive.

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:07.360
But if you use TPUs, it's
the same thing, even not

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:08.290
even any compute.

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:10.952
If you're using high performance
storage, for example,

00:25:10.952 --> 00:25:12.160
that can also break the bank.

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:13.690
So you be very
careful about loading

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:15.610
at these kinds of scales.

00:25:15.610 --> 00:25:19.750
And that is another example of
us showing that we can actually

00:25:19.750 --> 00:25:21.940
run through all of
these architecture steps

00:25:21.940 --> 00:25:26.190
and keep the GPU
utilization at or about 80%.

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:28.860
That alone is not a
simple problem to crack.

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:30.360
So when you're
thinking about scale,

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:33.330
also think about the fact
that if you acquire hardware

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:34.950
or acquire virtual
hardware, you also

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:37.180
need software that can
scale with the virtual

00:25:37.180 --> 00:25:39.150
or actually real hardware.

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:42.060
Now how did we get here?

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:44.320
The title of the talk
is democratizing AI.

00:25:44.320 --> 00:25:46.840
And I promise I'll
get back to that.

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:49.330
Doing all of these
things is not necessarily

00:25:49.330 --> 00:25:51.250
unique for oil and gas.

00:25:51.250 --> 00:25:53.650
Almost all industries
out there are solving

00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:56.130
one hard problem or the other.

00:25:56.130 --> 00:26:00.110
And to get to anything at
scale, you need more than one

00:26:00.110 --> 00:26:02.450
or two types of disciplines.

00:26:02.450 --> 00:26:06.560
And we got here by blood
and sweat and very hard work

00:26:06.560 --> 00:26:11.090
by a lot of people in very
disparate and different

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:11.820
communities.

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:13.520
So we had data scientists.

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:17.660
But lumping all data scientists
into one large bucket

00:26:17.660 --> 00:26:19.190
is also not being
that fair to them.

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:21.770
There are traditional
machine learning researchers.

00:26:21.770 --> 00:26:23.390
There are probabilistic
researchers.

00:26:23.390 --> 00:26:25.520
And also, a lot of deep
learning researchers

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:28.880
working together to get
us to where we are today.

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:32.660
Domain experts are the folks who
told us what to go solve for.

00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:34.700
So we didn't really go
to them and say, give us

00:26:34.700 --> 00:26:35.570
your easy problems.

00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:36.838
We'll just make them faster.

00:26:36.838 --> 00:26:38.630
We actually told them,
give us the problems

00:26:38.630 --> 00:26:41.120
that you can't solve or
have not been able to solve.

00:26:41.120 --> 00:26:44.420
Give us your hard problems
and help us understand them

00:26:44.420 --> 00:26:46.220
to the point where we
can at least tell you

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:47.900
whether we can
solve them or not.

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:50.480
And we walked into
problems where

00:26:50.480 --> 00:26:54.470
we had a certainty of maybe 50%
to think that we can solve it.

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:55.970
And in many cases
it is necessary,

00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:57.680
because those are
the problems that

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:01.520
could justify the cost of
using a system like this.

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:03.980
And software again, is
not all created equal.

00:27:03.980 --> 00:27:06.500
So we put these
specific words here

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:08.930
because thinking about
software in silos

00:27:08.930 --> 00:27:10.520
also causes a lot of problems.

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:13.160
Most of our software
engineers were full stack.

00:27:13.160 --> 00:27:16.220
They also were very experienced
in distributed systems.

00:27:16.220 --> 00:27:18.437
And I'll talk to
you about that why.

00:27:18.437 --> 00:27:20.270
And thinking about cloud
native is something

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:22.370
we had to learn along the way.

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:25.550
We started with systems that
were completely on prem,

00:27:25.550 --> 00:27:27.540
and then moved to
the cloud thinking,

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:29.240
OK, we can more
or less modify it

00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:31.320
a little bit to
get to the cloud.

00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:34.710
Only when we started hitting
scales of the problems

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:36.120
that we had to
solve, we realized

00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:38.590
that we had to go rewrite
a lot of different things.

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:40.560
So if you want things
that on that scale,

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:45.930
be willing to rewrite 30%, 40%,
sometimes 50% of your systems.

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:48.787
So what is
democratizing AI mean?

00:27:48.787 --> 00:27:50.870
Because I thought about
this a little bit for this

00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:53.430
talk, and I've been thinking
about this for a while.

00:27:53.430 --> 00:27:56.020
It means different things
to different people.

00:27:56.020 --> 00:27:59.730
Now if you're a data science
researcher, a data scientist,

00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:04.530
or a practitioner who's been
doing this for many years, when

00:28:04.530 --> 00:28:06.570
you hear the word
democratizing AI,

00:28:06.570 --> 00:28:09.660
you probably think OK, I
know how to build my models.

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:11.010
I have my tools.

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:12.450
I'm going to
continue doing that.

00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:16.650
I am very, very good about
using and tooling myself.

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:18.330
But I don't necessarily
know how to make

00:28:18.330 --> 00:28:21.090
my models available for hundreds
if not thousands of people

00:28:21.090 --> 00:28:22.510
repeatably.

00:28:22.510 --> 00:28:25.342
So productionizing
a model is probably

00:28:25.342 --> 00:28:27.300
what people think of when
you're a data science

00:28:27.300 --> 00:28:28.357
researcher or an expert.

00:28:28.357 --> 00:28:30.440
And by the way, I looked
up the word productionize

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:31.760
is a word apparently in--

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:37.050
If you're a domain
expert or a business user

00:28:37.050 --> 00:28:40.200
or somebody who hears
a lot about AI, who's

00:28:40.200 --> 00:28:42.540
heard about all the buzz,
maybe tried something

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:46.500
about on yourself, and find
that most of the tooling

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:48.180
is hard to use,
most of the jargon

00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:50.400
is very hard to understand,
and many times you

00:28:50.400 --> 00:28:53.970
can't connect a outcome that
looks nice to the outcome

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:55.560
that you care
about, then you want

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:58.350
to be able to build these
models fast and productionize

00:28:58.350 --> 00:28:59.532
very, very fast.

00:28:59.532 --> 00:29:00.990
Now if you're a
software developer,

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:03.070
if you're an
applications developer,

00:29:03.070 --> 00:29:05.940
you may not necessarily care
about the specific nuance

00:29:05.940 --> 00:29:09.600
of an algorithm or a specific
nuance of how the model has

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:13.680
been reduced to a size that can
be scaled and say, served well.

00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:15.360
Somebody already did that work.

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:17.460
You care more about
how do I consume it

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:20.140
for the particular use
case you have in mind.

00:29:20.140 --> 00:29:21.990
Now any of the tool
sets out there,

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:23.470
any other tooling
out there, people

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:26.130
who are building tools
for democratizing AI,

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:29.040
have to cater to all of
these three different very

00:29:29.040 --> 00:29:32.970
different say, workloads.

00:29:32.970 --> 00:29:35.460
And we've been doing
this for a while.

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:37.512
And mostly because of
necessity for ourselves,

00:29:37.512 --> 00:29:38.970
because we have a
small team, which

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:42.090
has to scale to very large
problems, as well as very, very

00:29:42.090 --> 00:29:43.410
large scale systems.

00:29:43.410 --> 00:29:45.330
So we made some very
careful choices.

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:49.860
So being container
first, was by necessity.

00:29:49.860 --> 00:29:53.260
And I will give you some metrics
on what going to containers

00:29:53.260 --> 00:29:54.120
did to us.

00:29:54.120 --> 00:29:56.040
So we did come up
with a philosophy

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:57.120
called depend on Docker.

00:29:57.120 --> 00:29:58.230
That's a logo for it.

00:29:58.230 --> 00:30:01.860
Obviously, we like doing things,
hence D-O, depend on Docker.

00:30:01.860 --> 00:30:05.220
And everything we
run is containerized.

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:10.170
Except for raw storage,
everything is containerized.

00:30:10.170 --> 00:30:11.940
And we also make
sure that that means

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:13.650
we have to orchestrate
at scale as well.

00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:19.980
So we'll show you a demo of
a model and a set of models

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:21.340
running at scale today.

00:30:21.340 --> 00:30:25.290
But before I get to the demos,
just what did it do to us?

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:28.050
So like, rewind
five years ago, when

00:30:28.050 --> 00:30:30.540
we were trying to build
what we called at that point

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:33.510
in time, a digital
twin platform for GE,

00:30:33.510 --> 00:30:35.130
we would build a
technique, we would

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:37.950
build a model that would
be in the research phase.

00:30:37.950 --> 00:30:39.980
Getting that model
into production

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:41.730
meant that we had to
stick to a very, very

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:44.670
strong say, quarterly releases.

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:47.340
And every single
quarterly release

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:50.440
would go up to the midnight
before the release,

00:30:50.440 --> 00:30:52.440
and something that was
working just fine the day

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:53.588
before would break.

00:30:53.588 --> 00:30:55.380
And most of the time
it was some dependency

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:57.240
around the model side.

00:30:57.240 --> 00:30:59.130
And the number of times
we heard hey, this

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:01.270
is working just
fine on my laptop

00:31:01.270 --> 00:31:03.930
or is working just fine on
my system, it's something

00:31:03.930 --> 00:31:05.690
wrong with the
production system, just

00:31:05.690 --> 00:31:08.490
drove us nuts to be honest.

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:09.448
We moved to containers.

00:31:09.448 --> 00:31:11.407
And five years ago moved
the containers was not

00:31:11.407 --> 00:31:11.980
that popular.

00:31:11.980 --> 00:31:14.700
We had to still face comments
like, well, nobody would ever

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:16.940
let you put run
containers in our system,

00:31:16.940 --> 00:31:20.070
to today when everybody run
everything in containers.

00:31:20.070 --> 00:31:22.080
It moved the
production deployment

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:24.690
of a model that has
already been validated,

00:31:24.690 --> 00:31:28.833
taking six months
to roughly an hour.

00:31:28.833 --> 00:31:31.000
So the model has already
been validated by somebody.

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:32.958
It's a data scientist,
a subject matter expert,

00:31:32.958 --> 00:31:37.510
they agree that model going
from being in their systems

00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:41.050
to a production system is
roughly an hour for us today.

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:44.210
Of course, it doesn't always
happen in one hour, every time.

00:31:44.210 --> 00:31:46.830
But if the data
scientist was say,

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:49.330
disciplined enough to use
our tooling from day one

00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:51.910
and actually have everything
running in containers, those

00:31:51.910 --> 00:31:54.940
containers transmitting
into our production systems

00:31:54.940 --> 00:31:56.860
is under an hour.

00:31:56.860 --> 00:31:59.320
So that's really what
going to containers

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:01.570
and sticking with
discipline got us.

00:32:01.570 --> 00:32:03.520
Now the demo use
case I'll show you

00:32:03.520 --> 00:32:06.925
two different types of flavors.

00:32:06.925 --> 00:32:08.800
The first one is a simple
case where I'm just

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:13.750
going to take a data set, run it
through some algorithms that'll

00:32:13.750 --> 00:32:17.470
detect what is in this data
set and tell you what possible

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:18.790
models we can build.

00:32:18.790 --> 00:32:21.520
On a UI, actually it will
build several different kinds

00:32:21.520 --> 00:32:25.340
of models to get
you to a best model.

00:32:25.340 --> 00:32:29.180
But the same demo will
also run and show you

00:32:29.180 --> 00:32:31.730
that this can scale
across thousands

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:33.342
of models built live on stage.

00:32:33.342 --> 00:32:35.300
And that was a promise
in the abstract as well.

00:32:35.300 --> 00:32:38.930
And hopefully, if the
demo gods agree today,

00:32:38.930 --> 00:32:40.940
we'll show the
demo working today.

00:32:40.940 --> 00:32:43.100
To get that to
work, we obviously

00:32:43.100 --> 00:32:44.600
need high speed storage.

00:32:44.600 --> 00:32:47.567
But a lot of the core
services had to scale as well.

00:32:47.567 --> 00:32:49.400
And we had to break it
down to a point where

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:53.880
we can control things that
are very fine grain level.

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:57.350
And if you see at the top
we have both GKE clusters

00:32:57.350 --> 00:32:59.425
and self-managed clusters.

00:32:59.425 --> 00:33:01.550
And in this particular
case, we did that because we

00:33:01.550 --> 00:33:02.660
were experimenting with things.

00:33:02.660 --> 00:33:04.202
But it actually came
back to save us,

00:33:04.202 --> 00:33:08.030
because the recent update
from wonderful team

00:33:08.030 --> 00:33:11.600
introduced a non backward
compatible change.

00:33:11.600 --> 00:33:14.490
And that broke our
self-managed clusters.

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:16.070
But the GKE clusters are up.

00:33:16.070 --> 00:33:17.780
So actually preparing
for this talk,

00:33:17.780 --> 00:33:19.850
we were fine actually
going through preparing

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:23.493
as we were upgrading our
self-managed classes as well.

00:33:23.493 --> 00:33:25.160
So I'll walk you
through how to do that.

00:33:25.160 --> 00:33:27.230
But this is the cluster
we'll be showing

00:33:27.230 --> 00:33:29.570
you hopefully live running.

00:33:29.570 --> 00:33:30.900
We have a master node.

00:33:30.900 --> 00:33:33.033
And what is expanded
on the right hand side

00:33:33.033 --> 00:33:33.950
is just a master node.

00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:35.840
The lines are not
necessarily correct.

00:33:35.840 --> 00:33:38.990
So we have 100
nodes per cluster.

00:33:38.990 --> 00:33:41.330
That's the GKE master cluster.

00:33:41.330 --> 00:33:44.030
On the top are five
workload clusters.

00:33:44.030 --> 00:33:46.580
On the bottom are five
self-managed clusters.

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:50.180
So I'm going to kick off
some model run times.

00:33:50.180 --> 00:33:54.570
And that'll actually load all of
these different cases as well.

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:58.640
So before I go run and spin
up all of those clusters,

00:33:58.640 --> 00:34:01.610
I just wanted to show you a
demo of what a subject matter

00:34:01.610 --> 00:34:03.950
expert would do if he
didn't know anything

00:34:03.950 --> 00:34:06.500
about what is in the data set.

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:10.489
So this is a AI system
called AI factory.

00:34:10.489 --> 00:34:14.510
But I just come in and
drag and drop a data set.

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:17.600
The data set is simple
tabular data, as you can see.

00:34:17.600 --> 00:34:20.730
It just has one header with,
in this particular case,

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:23.909
I think five variables and
about 100 rows of data.

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:26.420
And this is a data
set that we just

00:34:26.420 --> 00:34:29.360
created to tell you how
do you predict a wear

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:33.080
and tear of a wheel depending
on how fast you're accelerating

00:34:33.080 --> 00:34:35.030
in the car, what surface
you're driving on,

00:34:35.030 --> 00:34:36.056
and things like that.

00:34:36.056 --> 00:34:37.639
So what happened
here is saying, OK it

00:34:37.639 --> 00:34:39.409
knows that it's a CSV file.

00:34:39.409 --> 00:34:42.530
It wants to see if we can
build [INAUDIBLE] build.

00:34:42.530 --> 00:34:47.000
Now going from that first page
to here, what happened was it

00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:48.800
understood what is in the file.

00:34:48.800 --> 00:34:50.310
And this is granted,
a small file.

00:34:50.310 --> 00:34:52.352
But if we had a large file
with millions of rows,

00:34:52.352 --> 00:34:53.659
it'll do the same thing.

00:34:53.659 --> 00:34:56.270
It understood what are the
variables in the file that

00:34:56.270 --> 00:34:58.560
are numeric, and also
went in and said,

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:01.880
OK, given that these are
the variables you have,

00:35:01.880 --> 00:35:04.040
these are the potential
variables that you can

00:35:04.040 --> 00:35:07.930
use as outputs in your system.

00:35:07.930 --> 00:35:10.100
And that means that for
each of those outputs

00:35:10.100 --> 00:35:12.660
it will go in and use the rest
of the variables as inputs

00:35:12.660 --> 00:35:14.460
and try and build
your best model.

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:16.480
And it not only did that,
it went back and said,

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:18.840
OK, here are all the
different techniques

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:21.480
in the system they're
already preloaded that can

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:23.235
be used with this data set.

00:35:23.235 --> 00:35:25.508
Now if you're a
subject matter expert,

00:35:25.508 --> 00:35:28.050
you don't necessarily know what
all of these techniques mean.

00:35:28.050 --> 00:35:29.760
You don't necessarily
need to care.

00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:30.510
You just got here.

00:35:30.510 --> 00:35:32.220
The system is
suggesting something.

00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:34.133
Let it continue to
go on up to a point

00:35:34.133 --> 00:35:36.300
where it built some models
and then you can evaluate

00:35:36.300 --> 00:35:37.823
if the model is useful or not.

00:35:37.823 --> 00:35:38.990
That's we are going with it.

00:35:38.990 --> 00:35:42.780
Now if you don't necessarily
agree with all the variables

00:35:42.780 --> 00:35:44.760
that it shows, for
example, if I don't want

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:46.260
to build a model
for friction, but I

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:47.968
want to build the
model for acceleration,

00:35:47.968 --> 00:35:49.390
I could do that as well.

00:35:49.390 --> 00:35:52.560
And then I go in and say,
OK build all 24 models.

00:35:52.560 --> 00:35:56.070
Now a couple of things need
to happen for this to work.

00:35:56.070 --> 00:35:57.810
Where it needs to
take this data set,

00:35:57.810 --> 00:36:00.040
split that into
testing and training,

00:36:00.040 --> 00:36:02.280
connect it to all of these
different techniques,

00:36:02.280 --> 00:36:04.210
select all the
defaults, and then

00:36:04.210 --> 00:36:05.460
come out with the best models.

00:36:05.460 --> 00:36:08.220
Now in the time frame that
I just said that, it went in

00:36:08.220 --> 00:36:11.460
built all those models
live, came back and said

00:36:11.460 --> 00:36:13.380
it's telling us
what are the best

00:36:13.380 --> 00:36:17.490
models for each kind of output
that the system told us.

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:20.658
Now some of these outputs may
not necessarily make sense.

00:36:20.658 --> 00:36:22.200
For example, I don't
necessarily want

00:36:22.200 --> 00:36:24.090
to build a model for weight.

00:36:24.090 --> 00:36:25.560
But I want a model for where.

00:36:25.560 --> 00:36:27.960
I want a model for
acceleration, for example.

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:30.550
And it also went in and said
out of the six techniques

00:36:30.550 --> 00:36:33.600
it went and evaluated, for each
of the output, it's telling you

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:35.960
what's the best model based
on a particular root mean

00:36:35.960 --> 00:36:37.210
square [INAUDIBLE].

00:36:37.210 --> 00:36:38.580
It's a simple example.

00:36:38.580 --> 00:36:40.920
I can add it at
scale here on the UI.

00:36:40.920 --> 00:36:43.410
Now what if instead of
having six variables,

00:36:43.410 --> 00:36:46.200
you actually had
10,000 variables?

00:36:46.200 --> 00:36:47.990
Instead of building
24 models, you

00:36:47.990 --> 00:36:49.550
want to build 10,000 models?

00:36:49.550 --> 00:36:51.270
Or 100,000 models?

00:36:51.270 --> 00:36:54.090
So we have a CLI
for that as well.

00:36:54.090 --> 00:37:00.780
So let me switch to that and to
just walk you through this one.

00:37:00.780 --> 00:37:05.400
I have 10 cubed contexts
running on the left hand side.

00:37:05.400 --> 00:37:07.950
And each one-- so the
first left hand side ones

00:37:07.950 --> 00:37:09.730
are GKE clusters.

00:37:09.730 --> 00:37:12.290
So the GKE 1, 2,
all the way to 5.

00:37:12.290 --> 00:37:15.120
On the right hand side are
our self-managed Kubernetes

00:37:15.120 --> 00:37:16.030
clusters.

00:37:16.030 --> 00:37:17.520
So that's the tower you saw.

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:18.750
So the left hand side is GKE.

00:37:18.750 --> 00:37:21.370
Right hand side is self-managed.

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:26.030
And I'm going to just run
a simple watch command.

00:37:26.030 --> 00:37:27.530
So I'm going to
just watch the port.

00:37:30.630 --> 00:37:33.290
And most of it is telling you
that no researchers are found.

00:37:33.290 --> 00:37:35.623
That means nothing is actually
happening there because I

00:37:35.623 --> 00:37:36.960
have not done anything yet.

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:47.420
Now let me go in here and run
the case where I'm actually

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:50.120
spinning up the
same thing, where

00:37:50.120 --> 00:37:53.060
we are simulating 10
different users running

00:37:53.060 --> 00:37:55.400
1,000 models each.

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:58.130
So it's going to spin up
10,000 models simultaneously

00:37:58.130 --> 00:38:00.210
across all of these
different clusters.

00:38:00.210 --> 00:38:03.100
So if I had this cluster
and [INAUDIBLE] my fingers.

00:38:07.242 --> 00:38:09.700
What you're going to see is
different colors going through.

00:38:09.700 --> 00:38:13.880
So each color is one
particular Kubernetes cluster.

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:16.780
So it's actually loading all
of the Kubernetes clusters

00:38:16.780 --> 00:38:17.480
simultaneously.

00:38:17.480 --> 00:38:19.480
And what you're going to
see is on the left hand

00:38:19.480 --> 00:38:22.990
side, each of the
clusters actually starting

00:38:22.990 --> 00:38:25.488
to get loaded.

00:38:25.488 --> 00:38:27.030
And you can see some
of them started.

00:38:30.130 --> 00:38:31.390
And it's processing.

00:38:31.390 --> 00:38:35.530
Each of the nodes are going
to process about 100 models.

00:38:35.530 --> 00:38:40.500
If I go back to our system
that's monitoring all this--

00:38:48.230 --> 00:38:52.743
So what you're seeing are
all of the 10 clusters

00:38:52.743 --> 00:38:54.160
along with the
management cluster.

00:38:59.330 --> 00:39:03.180
And what you're seeing
is the blue dots

00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:06.210
are the management cluster,
the 10 Kubernetes clusters

00:39:06.210 --> 00:39:09.570
are around them, and the white
dots that are popping up in

00:39:09.570 --> 00:39:11.280
are the models that
are being built

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:14.160
live as I started building it.

00:39:14.160 --> 00:39:15.930
And the total number
of services that

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:18.300
are running to
make this possible

00:39:18.300 --> 00:39:20.233
is well over 1,000 services.

00:39:20.233 --> 00:39:21.900
And the jobs-- you
can see the job count

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:24.470
is actually going up live.

00:39:24.470 --> 00:39:27.530
So it looks like the demo gods
are finally smiling on us.

00:39:27.530 --> 00:39:30.900
Now we did build another
case where 100,000 models

00:39:30.900 --> 00:39:32.002
were running live.

00:39:32.002 --> 00:39:33.710
And I wanted to show
that system as well.

00:39:33.710 --> 00:39:35.627
And I'll come back and
show you these numbers.

00:39:38.390 --> 00:39:41.260
And this is a case
where all 100,000

00:39:41.260 --> 00:39:43.570
models ran and completed.

00:39:43.570 --> 00:39:51.790
And I can go back to this case,
reduce the speed a little bit.

00:39:51.790 --> 00:39:55.540
And adjust this size
also a little bit.

00:39:55.540 --> 00:40:01.830
So this is what a system
that builds 100,000 models

00:40:01.830 --> 00:40:02.640
look like.

00:40:02.640 --> 00:40:03.960
And this was done live.

00:40:03.960 --> 00:40:07.000
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:07.500
Thank you.

00:40:10.308 --> 00:40:12.445
Now if I go back
here, you can see

00:40:12.445 --> 00:40:13.820
the models are
still being built.

00:40:13.820 --> 00:40:15.230
It's still being submitted.

00:40:15.230 --> 00:40:16.970
And the interesting
piece here is

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:20.210
to get to this level of
scale, you can see all of them

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:21.227
have been submitted.

00:40:21.227 --> 00:40:23.060
Kubernetes are still
responding and actually

00:40:23.060 --> 00:40:24.600
kicking off the jobs.

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:26.570
So the load balancer
here not only had

00:40:26.570 --> 00:40:28.820
to watch what is a
load on the system,

00:40:28.820 --> 00:40:30.770
it also had to be
optimized to the level

00:40:30.770 --> 00:40:33.785
where it's watching which
of these clusters, which

00:40:33.785 --> 00:40:36.080
of these Kubernetes
clusters are having

00:40:36.080 --> 00:40:38.370
a delay in the responding back.

00:40:38.370 --> 00:40:40.700
So sometimes the Kubernetes
API takes two seconds

00:40:40.700 --> 00:40:42.830
to give us a response back.

00:40:42.830 --> 00:40:44.870
So in cases like that,
we won't necessarily

00:40:44.870 --> 00:40:46.328
go load up that
particular cluster.

00:40:46.328 --> 00:40:47.940
We'll move the load
to somewhere else.

00:40:47.940 --> 00:40:49.398
So it's not just
that it's watching

00:40:49.398 --> 00:40:52.070
what is happening
inside those clusters,

00:40:52.070 --> 00:40:54.180
it's also watching Kubernetes.

00:40:54.180 --> 00:40:55.723
Now you built all these models.

00:40:55.723 --> 00:40:57.390
You want to know what
is the best model.

00:40:57.390 --> 00:41:00.800
So you can run a query
across all of these models

00:41:00.800 --> 00:41:03.650
that just got built. It
will come back and tell you

00:41:03.650 --> 00:41:05.540
that it's not just one answer.

00:41:05.540 --> 00:41:07.728
So you need to know whether
it's a best model based

00:41:07.728 --> 00:41:09.020
on what root mean square error.

00:41:09.020 --> 00:41:10.860
That was what we
showed on the UI.

00:41:10.860 --> 00:41:12.950
But you can also say that
is the best model based

00:41:12.950 --> 00:41:15.500
on max percentage error or
average percentage error,

00:41:15.500 --> 00:41:17.390
or any of the different
percentages errors

00:41:17.390 --> 00:41:20.570
that you care about as a data
scientist or a subject matter

00:41:20.570 --> 00:41:21.950
expert as well.

00:41:21.950 --> 00:41:24.050
And this is coming
live from the system

00:41:24.050 --> 00:41:25.820
that is actually running there.

00:41:25.820 --> 00:41:29.890
And you can see that jobs are
continuing to be submitted.

00:41:29.890 --> 00:41:31.600
There you go.

00:41:31.600 --> 00:41:33.970
So we did show you real
world use cases that

00:41:33.970 --> 00:41:36.130
require a lot of great scale.

00:41:36.130 --> 00:41:38.345
And getting to
scale requires you

00:41:38.345 --> 00:41:39.970
to think slightly
differently, and also

00:41:39.970 --> 00:41:42.010
think about scale from day one.

00:41:42.010 --> 00:41:44.740
Thinking about scale
at day 10 or day 100

00:41:44.740 --> 00:41:47.980
causes a lot more angst than
thinking about it on day one.

00:41:47.980 --> 00:41:50.450
And we had to learn
that painfully

00:41:50.450 --> 00:41:51.950
on many of our
applications as well.

00:41:51.950 --> 00:41:55.240
But what we showed you today
was using Docker, Kubernetes,

00:41:55.240 --> 00:42:00.010
Kubeflow, GCP, NGC
containers in a way that

00:42:00.010 --> 00:42:01.630
is significantly scalable.

00:42:01.630 --> 00:42:04.190
And it's definitely
scalable, but it takes

00:42:04.190 --> 00:42:05.440
quite a bit of effort as well.

00:42:05.440 --> 00:42:07.720
So I want to end
this with credits

00:42:07.720 --> 00:42:10.210
from the team that's
listed here and many, many

00:42:10.210 --> 00:42:15.033
more who worked tirelessly for
several years to get us here.

00:42:15.033 --> 00:42:15.950
So thanks a lot, guys.

00:42:15.950 --> 00:42:16.450
Thank you.

00:42:16.450 --> 00:42:19.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

